{"step_index":1093,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-14T03:05:49Z","input_tokens":2458,"cache_read_tokens":37992,"output_tokens":2550,"thinking":"`.env.example` requires updates to document Google OAuth credentials and application URL, along with email configuration. A dedicated module, `src/lib/google-auth.ts`, will encapsulate the Google OAuth logic. The initial focus is on building a function to reliably detect the application's base URL.\n\n\nDetermining the base URL for the application is critical for constructing the Google OAuth redirect URI; this requires checking an environment variable first, then falling back to request headers if available, and finally defaulting to `localhost:3000`. Next, a function is defined to check if the Google OAuth credentials have been set in the environment.\n\nThe Google OAuth URL generation involves constructing the authorization URL using provided base URL and a state parameter, incorporating the required scopes, and properly forming the query string with necessary parameters. The next step involves exchanging the authorization code for tokens and fetching user information.\n\n\nA function is defined to exchange the authorization code received from Google with access and ID tokens, essential for user authentication and authorization. Error handling is included to manage scenarios where token exchange or user information retrieval fails.  The function then fetches user information using the access token and returns a structured object containing relevant user details.\n\n\nThe initial request handler checks for a \"demo\" parameter or unconfigured Google OAuth credentials, redirecting to a demo mode if either condition is met. If neither, it constructs a state object, base64 encodes it, generates the Google OAuth URL using the retrieved base URL and encoded state, and then redirects the user to this authorization URL.\n\nThe callback route receives authorization code and potential errors from Google. It retrieves the base URL and checks for errors or missing codes, redirecting with an error message if any exist. Then, if a state parameter is available it tries to decode it and extract the intended user role.\n\n\nFirst, the callback route attempts to extract the user's intended role from the decoded state. Then, Google user information is fetched using the authorization code and the base URL. The process then checks for an existing user in the system based on their Google ID. If no existing user is found by Google ID, a search is performed using their email.\n\nIf a user exists with the email, their Google ID is linked to it. Otherwise, a new user is created. Parent users receive a unique family code.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"\"e:\\\\codekids\\\\src\\\\app\\\\dashboard\\\\layout.tsx\"","toolAction":"\"Viewing dashboard layout\"","toolSummary":"\"View dashboard layout\""}}]}
